  "body": "## Purpose\n\nFor hybrid models running `mamba_cache_mode=\"align\"`, the Mamba group retains only a sparse set of state checkpoints. When none of them lands inside a shared prefix, `HybridKVCacheCoordinator` intersects the per-group hits and the attention groups' matches are discarded. Prefix caching then reports a clean miss, which is indistinguishable from \"these requests simply don't share a prefix\" — there is no signal that reuse was found and then thrown away.\n\nThe coordinator already computes the size of that loss (`num_uncached_common_prefix_tokens`) in order to pin the Marconi shared-prefix junction, and then drops it. This PR records it in the prefix cache stats and exports it as `vllm:prefix_cache_sparse_retention_misses`.\n\nThis is observability only. Scheduling, allocation, lookup, and model output are unchanged.\n\nRelates to #45238.\n\n### Why this matters more now\n\n- #50991 enabled Mamba prefix caching by default.\n- #52216 promotes `prefix_cache_retention_interval` to an argument and defaults it to `0`, i.e. sparser retention by default.\n\nBoth increase how often a lookup can lose a match this way, and neither makes the loss visible.\n\nThere is also a concrete remedy once the signal exists. `--prefix-match-unit` enables fine-grained partial Mamba hits, but its default is the GCD of the group block sizes; in align mode the attention block size is set equal to the Mamba block size, so the default GCD equals `block_size`, the `mamba.block_size > hash_block_size` gate in `enable_partial_hash_hits` is false, and the mechanism is inert unless an operator explicitly sets the flag. Measured on this branch with a 2-group align hybrid at `block_size=544`:\n\n| config | `hash_block_size` | partial Mamba hits |\n|---|---|---|\n| default (`prefix_match_unit=None`) | 544 | disabled |\n| `--prefix-match-unit 136` | 136 | enabled |\n\nSo today an operator has the knob but no indication they need it. This counter is what tells them.\n\n## Why this is not a duplicate\n\n- No open PR references #45238, and none exposes this quantity. Checked with `gh pr list --search \"45238 in:body\"`, plus keyword sweeps over `mamba prefix cache`, `mamba cache mode align`, `VLLM_PREFIX_CACHE_RETENTION_INTERVAL`, and `prefix cache metric observability`.\n- #52216 and #50551 / #49574 change **which** checkpoints are retained. This reports the cost when none is reachable, so they compose rather than compete — as #52216 makes retention sparser by default, this signal becomes more useful, not redundant.\n- #51807 adds coverage for prefix-cache metric accounting under same-step Mamba deferral (that each request is counted once). It does not surface vetoed hits.\n- #47491 attempted to *fix* the veto by letting attention hits survive a Mamba miss and was closed as a correctness hazard (`has_initial_states` is derived from the scalar `num_computed_tokens`, so the resume boundary would have no valid state). This PR deliberately does not change hit semantics — it only reports the loss.\n\n## Test plan\n\n```bash\nuv venv --python 3.12\nVLLM_USE_PRECOMPILED=1 uv pip install -e . --torch-backend=auto\n\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/v1/core/test_prefix_caching.py \\\n  tests/v1/core/prefix_cache/ tests/v1/core/test_kv_cache_utils.py \\\n  tests/v1/core/test_scheduler.py -q\n\npre-commit run --files vllm/v1/metrics/stats.py vllm/v1/metrics/loggers.py \\\n  vllm/v1/core/kv_cache_manager.py tests/v1/core/test_prefix_caching.py \\\n  tests/entrypoints/serve/instrumentator/test_metrics.py docs/design/metrics.md\n```\n\n## Test result\n\n`348 passed, 1 failed`. The single failure is `test_async_scheduling_pp_allows_rescheduling_with_output_placeholders`, which fails identically on the unmodified base commit (`4d2a68d64`) — verified by checking out the base and running it in isolation. It is unrelated to this change.\n\nAll `pre-commit` hooks pass, including `mypy`.\n\nTwo unit tests are added to `tests/v1/core/test_prefix_caching.py`: one asserting the counter reports the lost tokens when an align-mode Mamba group holds no checkpoint at the shared-prefix junction, and one asserting it stays at zero on a hit every group agrees on. As a negative control, stubbing the computation to `0` makes the first test fail (expected 32, got 0), confirming it exercises the new path rather than passing vacuously.\n\n### End to end\n\n`Qwen3.5-4B` (AWQ 4-bit), single GPU, `--enable-prefix-caching --enforce-eager`, `--max-model-len 8192`, concurrency 1, 32 prompts over 8 prefix groups. Engine selected `mamba_cache_mode=\"align\"` and `block_size=528`, matching the geometry in #45238. Workloads are `vllm bench serve --dataset-name prefix_repetition`, differing only in where the 1584-token block boundary falls:\n\n| | 1600 / 600 (boundary inside shared prefix) | 1500 / 700 (boundary in unique tokens) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `prefix_cache_queries` | 70,400 | 70,400 |\n| `prefix_cache_hits` | 38,016 (54.0%) | 17,952 (25.5%) |\n| **`prefix_cache_sparse_retention_misses`** | **0** | **8,448** |\n| Mean TTFT | 245.95 ms | 300.25 ms |\n\nThe counter stays at exactly zero on the healthy workload and reports 8,448 tokens (16 × 528, whole blocks) on the geometry-unlucky one, alongside a halved hit rate and a 22% TTFT regression that is otherwise unattributable.\n\nNote the unlucky case is 25.5%, not 0% — the Marconi admission policy (#37898) recovers most of it. The catastrophic case originally described in #45238 is genuinely fixed on current main; what remains is a real but silent residual loss, which is what this counter exposes.\n\n## Model evaluation\n\nNot applicable. This PR adds a counter and does not touch scheduling, block allocation, cache lookup, or anything reaching the model, so it cannot affect output or accuracy. No eval was run for that reason.\n\n## AI assistance\n\nThis change was developed with AI assistance (Claude, Fable 5). The investigation, patch, and tests were AI-produced; I reviewed every changed line, ran the test commands above, and can defend the change end to end.\n\ncc @njhill (authored #47782, the Marconi retention pinning this reports against) and @tlrmchlsmth (#52216, which defaults retention to sparse).\n",
